# Security basics

## Secrets

- Never commit or paste secrets (API keys, passwords, tokens, private keys).
- Don’t put secrets in client bundles (`NEXT_PUBLIC_*`, frontend env, repo docs).
- If a secret appears in chat or logs, warn and rotate — don’t repeat it.

## Auth & data access

- Enforce authorization on the server (or BFF), not only in the UI.
- Treat all client input as untrusted: validate/sanitize on the server.
- Prefer parameterized queries / ORM APIs — never string-concatenate SQL.
- Don’t log tokens, passwords, or full PII payloads.

## Web / API

- Use CSRF protections where cookie sessions apply; prefer bearer tokens over putting secrets in URLs.
- Set safe defaults for redirects (allowlist destinations).
- Avoid `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` / `eval` / dynamic `new Function` unless strictly necessary and sanitized.
- When handling files: validate type/size; store outside web roots when possible; serve via controlled URLs.

## Dependencies & exploits

- Don’t invent crypto; use standard libraries.
- Never write exploit PoCs, malware, or attack scripts — even “for testing.”
- Fix vulnerabilities in local code; don’t ship payloads.
